<description>
# Scherz &amp; Monk fig 8.42 — Overvoltage Protection (Crowbar) with op amp and SCR (book p. 660, PDF p. 689)

## What it shows

The op-amp crowbar from the "Overvoltage Protection (Crowbar)" figure. A +6 V supply feeds the
load through a fuse/breaker. R1 (10 k) and R2 (10 k trimmer, "adj.") divide the output down to
the op amp's **+** input; R3 (430 Ω) feeds a 3 V zener (cathode up) that clamps the op amp's
**−** input at 3.00 V. At the nominal 6 V both inputs sit at ~3 V (book: "the op amp's
differential input voltage is therefore zero"), the output is low, and the SCR — anode on the
output rail, cathode through the **reset** switch to ground — is off. When the output rises past
the trip point the divider lifts the **+** input above 3 V, the comparator output fires the SCR
gate, and the SCR crowbars the output to ground. The SCR then holds itself on through anode
current (the comparator only had to pulse the gate), diverting the fault until the fuse blows
or power is removed; the book: *"the fuse (breaker) blows and the load is saved. The switch is
opened to reset the SCR."*

## Parts and values

Book values: **R1** 10 K, **R2** 10 K trimmer, **R3** 430 Ω, **V_zener** 3 V, SCR (no type),
fuse/breaker, "reset" switch. The figure draws no op amp type, no supplies, no load, and no
component values for the supply.

Chosen / inferred:
- **V1** battery 6 V, `Resistance` **2 Ω** as the supply's output impedance [INFERRED, same
  convention as books/scherz/fig11_15_overvoltage_crowbar.sim1] so the fired crowbar draws a
  finite fault current instead of hundreds of amps (MODEL_LIMITS #191).
- **RL** **600 Ω** load on the figure's +/− output terminals [INFERRED] (10 mA at 6 V).
- **F1** switch stands in for the fuse/breaker — SimulIDE has no fuse part (#316); closed =
  intact, open = tripped. A real 0.5 A fuse would blow instantly at the measured 2.6–3 A fault.
- **SW1** the "reset" switch, drawn closed; opens to interrupt the SCR's cathode current.
- **U1** op amp used open-loop as the comparator (the documented stand-in, MODEL_LIMITS #314),
  powered from the protected rail (`Power_Pins=true`, V+ to the rail via `Switch_Pins=true`),
  `Gain` **100**. With the default Gain 1000 the model's output-low clamp let go against the SCR
  gate junction after firing: the gate node escaped to ±kV and the "latched" SCR was kicked out
  again (see limits). Gain 100 clamps cleanly and still trips within ~40 mV.
- **Q1** generic SCR, SimulIDE defaults (TrigCurr 10 mA, HoldCurr 8.2 mA).

**R2** is wired as the figure draws it: a two-terminal adjustable resistor from the R1/R2
junction to ground (wiper tied to the bottom end), so A–B = Value_Ohm and the dial moves the
trip. At the book's setting (10 k, dial 100 %) the divider is 1:1 and the trip sits at
2 × V_zener ≈ 6.05 V; dialing down raises the trip (R_AB = 5 k → trip ≈ 9 V).

## How to use

Run at 6 V: the load sees the supply (5.97 V), tap and zener node both at ~3.0 V, SCR off.
Scope: **CH0** = output rail (yellow), **CH1** = comparator output/SCR gate (cyan),
**CH2** = divider tap (magenta); 500 ms/div, 2 V/div (CH2 1 V/div).

Raise V1 past ~6.06 V (or dial R2 while above the trip): the comparator output swings to ~6 V,
the SCR fires, the output collapses to ~0.9 V and the supply pushes ~3 A into the short. Lower
the supply back to 6 V: the output **stays** collapsed — gate at 0 V, SCR still on: latched.
Open SW1: the cathode current is interrupted, the fault is diverted and the rail recovers.
Full reset: remove power (V1 → 0) and re-apply, or (in this model) a fresh start. The R2 dial
sets the trip: 100 % → ≈ 6.05 V, 50 % → ≈ 9 V.

## Expected vs simulated [SIMULATED]

| Stage | Supply | Output (CH0) | Gate (CH1) | SCR | Fault / load current |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Normal | 6.00 V | **5.966 V**, load 9.94 mA | 0 V | OFF | 17.1 mA total |
| Trip scan | 6.06 V | 6.025 V | 0 V | OFF | — |
| **Trip** | 6.08 V | fires | fires | ON | — |
| Fired @ 7 V | 7.00 V | **collapses to 0.899 V** | 0 V after firing | ON | **3.05 A** (battery sags to 0.90 V) |
| Latched, surge passes | back to 6.00 V | **still 0.884 V** | 0 V (comparator low, tap 0.45 V) | ON at 2.56 A | load gets 1.47 mA |
| Reset switch open | 6.00 V | recovers to 5.86 V | 0 V | current interrupted | fault gone (68.8 mA residual) |
| Power removed | 0 V | 0 V | — | OFF | 0 |
| Power back | 6.00 V | 5.966 V | 0 V | OFF — normal again | 9.94 mA |
| R2 dialed 50 % (trip ≈ 9 V) | 7.00 V surge | 6.958 V — **does not fire** (tap 2.32 V &lt; 3.01 V) | 0 V | OFF | 21.2 mA |
| R2 back to 100 % | 7.00 V | comparator crosses → fires → 0.899 V | — | ON | 3.05 A |

**Trip voltage vs book:** fires between **6.06 and 6.08 V** input. Theory: trip when the tap
crosses the 3.009 V zener reference — V_trip = 3.009 × (R1+R2)/R2 = 6.018 V at the rail, plus
~40 mV of comparator overdrive (Gain 100 needs ~20 mV to swing the output far enough for
10 mA of gate current) ≈ 6.06 V at V1 including the 35 mV drop across the 2 Ω. Within one
50 mV step of the estimate.

**Latching:** after the collapse the tap falls to 0.45 V, the comparator output sits at 0 V —
the gate is no longer driven — and the SCR still carries 3.05 A, 370× its 8.2 mA holding
current. That is the crowbar's point: the comparator only pulses the gate; the SCR holds
itself, and the short persists until the anode path is interrupted (SW1) or the power is
removed. Measured stable across 4 s of continued runs and through the 7 V → 6 V transition.

**Op-amp trip vs the zener crowbar (fig 11.15):** the zener version trips where the rail passes
the zener knee **plus** the ~0.6 V gate threshold — measured 6.20–6.25 V against a 6.2 V
estimate, but the threshold is the sum of two soft, tolerance-dominated edges (a 5.6 V zener's
knee moves with current and ±5 % tolerance; the 0.6 V gate figure is a rough low-current
estimate). The op-amp version trips where a resistor ratio meets one fixed reference:
6.018 V by construction, measured 6.06–6.08 V, and **adjustable** over the whole range with the
R2 trimmer without touching the reference. The old caveat that simulated zener knees are too
soft to trip on (#232-era) is now moot — in today's models both circuits trip sharply — but the
bench argument stands: the op amp trades a zener's knee slop for a ratio-set, trimmable
threshold, at the cost of a supply-powered comparator that dies with the rail it protects.

## Simulator notes

- U1 `Gain` 100, `Power_Pins=true` + `Switch_Pins=true` (V+ to the rail): see limits. With
  Gain 1000 the fired state could land on a nonphysical branch (gate node at ±kV, latch
  oscillating, and one `comp set` on a latched circuit at 2.8 A froze the solver at 100 % CPU).
- The SCR model keeps its ON flag while the cathode is open: opening SW1 restores the rail but
  closing it again re-latches; only power removal fully clears the flag.
- After a source step the scope's endpoint samples can read stale values (2 samples, #183):
  measure settled windows (`--last 500 ms`) after each change, as the table above does.

## create-circuit skill

- Version: 1.20.1 (commit a75fa3b)
- Circuit: fig8_42_overvoltage_protection.sim1

### Limitations found while building it

- op amp output-low clamp fails against the SCR gate junction (Gain 1000): [#368](https://github.com/jeroenwk/create-circuit/issues/368)
- no fuse part (#316): [#369](https://github.com/jeroenwk/create-circuit/issues/369)
- SCR ON flag persists while the cathode is open: [#370](https://github.com/jeroenwk/create-circuit/issues/370)
</description>


